"make vcheck"
	will do many more tests.
        this test suite is moderately complete. There are still some options
        without tests, but all crucial ones are covered.

"make qa"
        will run something similar to
             configure && make clean && make && make vcheck
        multiple times:
        - gcc and/and clang, if available, multiplied with
        - ASan, MSan und UBSan.
        and a final coverage run.
        
        If this runs through, a final
             configure && make clean && make
        is run.

The checks will ./tests, and this directory will not be removed
after the check.
In it you will find, among things i didn't clean up:
- passfail.log (shows all tests run with PASSED/FAILED info)
- failures.log (shows all failed tests)
- perf.log (performance data)
- journal.log

Note that the tests are run over a pipe and a named pipe. The performance
values are in no way comparable to a real serial interface (or even a 
network connection).
Having said that: in real life the number will change, but what is faster
in the benchmark will most likely be faster over a real serial line.


If you want to do compatibility checks, note this:
    available="lrzsz"
    if test -x ~/bin/lrzsz-0.12.21rc/lrz -a -x ~/bin/lrzsz-0.12.21rc/lsz ; then
        available="$available lrzsz_old"
    fi
    if test -x ~/bin/crzsz/crz -a -x ~/bin/crzsz/csz ; then
        available="$available crzsz"
    fi
    if test -x ~/bin/rzsz373/rz -a -x ~/bin/rzsz373/sz ; then
        available="$available rzsz373"
    fi
    if test -x ~/bin/zmtx-zmrx-1.02/zmtx -a -x ~/bin/zmtx-zmrx-1.02/zmrx ; then
        available="$available zmtxzmrx102"
    fi
    if test -x ~/bin/zmtx-zmrx-2.02/zmtx -a -x ~/bin/zmtx-zmrx-2.02/zmrx ; then
        available="$available zmtxzmrx202"
    fi
    if test -x ~/bin/sexyz ; then
        available="$available sexyz"
    fi

zmtx-zmrx-2.02 is easy to compile.
sexyz is easy to compile.
The other are mostly historic, anyway.

oh, yes… "make vcheck-zbenchmark" runs a benchmark.

"make fuzz"
        runs the ZMODEM protocol fuzzing (src/zmodemfuzz.c).
        default: 25 iterations per mode (override with FUZZITER=NNN, 
           for example FUZZITER=200 make fuzz):
        - "-m receive": a faked sender talks to ./src/lrz with mutated
          byte streams (CRC corruption, illegal ZDLE escapes, frame type
          roulette, garbage floods, CAN bursts, truncated streams,
          hostile file names).
        - "-m send": a faked receiver answers ./src/lsz with hostile
          frame sequences (silence, ZNAK loops, ZSKIP, ZABORT, ZFERR,
          ZCHALLENGE, absurd ZRPOS positions, cancel bursts).
        The fuzzer checks that the target terminates in a bounded time,
        dies by no signal, writes nothing outside the receiving
        directory, and (clean baseline exchanges, every 17th iteration)
        that the received content matches what was sent. Every failure
        leaves /tmp/zmodemfuzz-fail-seedNNN-itNNN.log with the complete
        byte transcript; reproduce any run exactly with the printed seed:
             src/zmodemfuzz -s SEED -m receive -- ./src/lrz -q -Z
